      </section>

      <section class="editorial-chapter">
        <h2>Career Advancement Schemes: Time-Bound Advancement (TBA) &amp; Stagnation</h2>
        <p>
          To alleviate promotional bottlenecks across operational and clerical cadres, the Government of Karnataka implements established career advancement rules:
        </p>
        <div class="callout-box">
          <h4>Time-Bound Advancement (TBA at 10, 15, 20, 25 Years):</h4>
          <p>
            Under Karnataka Civil Services (Time Bound Advancement) Rules, employees who complete 10, 15, 20, or 25 years of continuous service in a single cadre without promotion receive automatic financial upgradation to the next promotional pay scale.
          </p>
          <p>
            Furthermore, employees who reach the maximum ceiling of their pay scale are awarded <strong>Stagnation Increments</strong> (up to five biennial increments), ensuring that long-serving senior staff continue to receive compensation increases even when awaiting promotional vacancies.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="editorial-chapter">
        <h2>Pension Architecture: NPS vs OPS &amp; KGID Social Security</h2>
        <p>
          Retirement compensation in Karnataka involves unique state-level welfare programs:
        </p>
        <ul class="bullet-feature-list">
          <li><strong>The Pension System Debate:</strong> State employees recruited after April 1, 2006 are enrolled under the National Pension System (NPS), contributing 10% of their Basic Pay &plus; DA. Employee federations continue active campaigns advocating for the restoration of the defined-benefit <strong>Old Pension Scheme (OPS)</strong> or the state adoption of the <strong>Unified Pension Scheme (UPS)</strong> model.</li>
          <li><strong>State Pensioner Revisions:</strong> Over 4.2 lakh retired state civil servants and family pensioners will see their baseline monthly pension multiplied by the approved 8th CPC fitment factor, elevating the statutory minimum state pension to over <strong>₹20,520 per month</strong> under a 2.28 factor.</li>
          <li><strong>Karnataka Government Insurance Department (KGID):</strong> Compulsory state life insurance policies operate alongside the state health scheme (Arogya Sanjeevini Scheme), providing comprehensive social security deductions directly on electronic pay slips.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="editorial-chapter">
        <h2>Digital Payroll Integration via the Khajane II Portal</h2>
        <p>
          Karnataka administers public wage disbursements through its robust digital financial network:
        </p>
        <p>
          The <strong>Khajane II Treasury Portal (khajane2.karnataka.gov.in)</strong> and the Human Resource Management System (HRMS 2.0) automate monthly salary processing, annual 3% increments, DA arrears, and pension remittances across all 31 districts of Karnataka.
        </p>
        <p>
          When the 8th Pay Commission Government Orders are officially gazetted by the Finance Department, revised pay scales and retrospective arrears will be automated directly through the electronic Khajane II network, guaranteeing seamless bank transfers for all government servants.
        </p>
      </section>

